        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\cos20^\\circ$ is irrational.\n\nThe only possible rational roots of $8x^3-6x-1=0$ are $\\pm1,\\pm\\frac12,\\pm\\frac14,\\pm\\frac18$; none satisfies the equation.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=8x^3-6x-1$. By the rational root theorem, the only possible rational roots are $\\pm1,\\pm\\frac12,\\pm\\frac14,\\pm\\frac18$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1, &P(-1)&=-3\\\\P\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=-3, &P\\left(-\\frac12\\right)&=1\\\\P\\left(\\frac14\\right)&=-\\frac{19}{8}, &P\\left(-\\frac14\\right)&=\\frac38\\\\P\\left(\\frac18\\right)&=-\\frac{111}{64}, &P\\left(-\\frac18\\right)&=-\\frac{17}{64}\\end{align*}$$\nNone is zero, so $P(x)=0$ has no rational roots. By parts (a) and (b), $\\cos20^\\circ$ is a root of $P(x)=0$. Hence $\\cos20^\\circ$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
